'''HMS ''Cossack''''' was a [[Tribal-class destroyer (1905)|Tribal-class]] [[destroyer]] of the [[Royal Navy]] launched in 1907 and sold in 1919.

==Construction and design==
HMS ''Cossack'' was one of five Tribal-class destroyers ordered as part of the 1905–06 shipbuilding programme.<ref name="friedman p108">{{harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=108}}</ref> While the [[British Admiralty|Admiralty]] laid down the basic requirements of an oil-fuelled, [[steam turbine]]-powered ship with a speed of {{convert|33|kn}}, a range of {{convert|1500|nmi}} at cruising speed and an endurance of eight hours at full speed, the details of the design of individual ships was left to the builders, which meant that individual ships of the class differed significantly from each other. Cammell Laird's design was powered by steam turbines rated at {{convert|14000|shp|kW}} fed by five boilers to drive three propeller shafts, and had three large funnels. Armament was the specified three [[QF 12 pounder 12 cwt naval gun|12 pounder (3 inch, 76 mm) 12 cwt guns]]{{#tag:ref|cwt stands for [[hundredweight]], 12 cwt referring to the weight of the gun.|group=lower-alpha}}, two side by side on the ship's [[forecastle]] and one aft, with two 18 in (450&nbsp;mm) torpedo tubes.<ref name="friedman p106-8">{{harvnb|Friedman|2009|pp=106–108}}</ref><ref name="conways06 p72">{{Harvnb|Gardiner|Gray|1985|p=72}}.</ref><ref name="friedman p100">{{Harvnb|Friedman|2009|p= 100}}</ref>

''Cossack'' was laid down on 13 November 1905 and launched on 16 February 1907.<ref name="friedman p305">{{harvnb|Friedman|2009|p=305}}</ref> After successfully undergoing trials<ref>{{cite magazine|url=http://www.gracesguide.co.uk/images/6/61/Er19080110a.pdf|title=Progress of Warships and Machinery Under Construction in England|magazine=[[The Engineer (UK magazine)|The Engineer]]|date=10 January 1908|volume= 105|page=29}}</ref> where she reached a speed of {{convert|34.619|kn}},<ref name="friedman p108"/> ''Cossack'' was commissioned in April 1908.<ref name="friedman p305"/>

==Service==
Shortly after ''Cossack'' entered service, it was decided to strengthen the armament of the first batch of Tribals by adding another two 12 pounder guns, this being done in 1909.<ref name="conways06 p72"/><ref name="friedman p109-0">{{harvnb|Friedman|2009|pp=109–110}}</ref> ''Cossack'' was considered by her Captain to be a poorer sea boat than [[River-class destroyer (1903)|River-class destroyer]]s,{{#tag:ref|A general problem with the seakeeping of the Tribals was that their forecastle was too short, so that at high speed, the ship's [[bow wave]] would wash over the deck.<ref name="burt p22">{{harvnb|Burt|1986|p=22}}</ref>|group=lower-alpha}} but a better gun platform.<ref name="friedman p109-0"/>

In 1909, following a re-organisation of the Royal Navy's [[Home Fleet|Home]] and [[Atlantic Fleet (United Kingdom)|Atlantic Fleet]]s, ''Cossack'' was part of the [[First Destroyer Flotilla]] of the Home Fleet.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Home and Atlantic Fleets |newspaper=[[The Times]] |date=15 March 1909 |issue= 38907 |page=9}}</ref>  1910, ''Cossack'' remained part of the First Flotilla in 1910.<ref name="nmm">{{cite web|url=http://www.rmg.co.uk/upload/pdf/Warship_Histories_Vessels_v.pdf|title=NMM, vessel ID 382853|work=Warship Histories, vol v|publisher=[[National Maritime Museum]]|accessdate=18 December 2013|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924092906/http://www.rmg.co.uk/sites/default/files/media/pdf//Warship_Histories_Vessels_v.pdf|archivedate=24 September 2015}}</ref> By 1913, she had joined the [[4th Destroyer Flotilla]],<ref name="nmm"/> based at [[HMNB Portsmouth|Portsmouth]].<ref name="manning p25">{{harvnb|Manning|1961|p=25}}</ref> In October that year, the Tribals were officially designated the F class, and as such the letter "F" was painted on ''Cossack''{{'}}s bows.<ref name="conways06 p72"/><ref name="friedman p100"/> While still a part of the 4th Flotilla in January 1914,<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Fleets and Squadrons in Commission at Home and Abroad: Flotillas of the First Fleet |magazine=The Navy List |date=January 1914 |page=269a |url=https://digital.nls.uk/british-military-lists/archive/91807093 |accessdate=16 May 2020 |via=National Library of Scotland}}</ref> but by February had transferred to the [[6th Destroyer Flotilla]].<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Fleets and Squadrons in Commission at Home and Abroad: Patrol Flotillas |magazine=The Navy List |date=February 1914 |page=269d |url=https://digital.nls.uk/british-military-lists/archive/91820645 |accessdate=16 May 2020 |via=National Library of Scotland}}</ref>

During the [[First World War]] she served in the [[North Sea]] and the [[English Channel]] with the 6th Destroyer Flotilla.

On 23 August 1914, ''Cossack'' was involved in a collision with her sister Tribal-class destroyer, [[HMS Ghurka (1907)|''Ghurka'']].<ref>{{cite web|last=Helgason|first=Guðmundur|url=http://www.uboat.net/wwi/ships_hit/2448.html|title=Ships hit during WWI: HMS Ghurka|work=u-boat.net|year=2013|accessdate=18 December 2013}}</ref> In October 1914, ''Cossack'' was one of a number of warships of the [[Dover Patrol]] that were deployed to help support Belgian ground forces during the [[Battle of the Yser]], with all available ships being used to carry out shore bombardment operations. At one stage, on 20 October 1914, after the destroyer [[HMS Amazon (1908)|''Amazon'']] was damaged by German shellfire, Rear Admiral [[Horace Hood]] transferred his flag to ''Cossack''.<ref>{{cite web|last=Corbett|first=Julian S.|title=History of the Great War: Naval Operations Vol. I To the Battle of the Falklands December 1914 (Part 1 of 2)| publisher=Naval-History.net|year=2013|orig-year=Originally published by Longmans, Green and Co.: London, 1920|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1Book-RN1a.htm|accessdate=18 December 2013}}</ref><ref name="baconv2 p611-4">{{harvnb|Bacon|1918|pp=611–614}}</ref>

On the night of 26/27 October 1916, German [[torpedo boat]]s of their [[Flanders Flotilla]] carried out a [[Battle of Dover Strait (1916)|large scale raid]] into the English Channel, hoping to attack the drifters watching the anti-submarine nets of the Dover Barrage, and to sink Allied shipping in the Channel. ''Cossack'' was one of six Tribal-class destroyers waiting at readiness in Dover harbour, and when the Germans attacked the drifters and sank the supporting destroyer {{HMS|Flirt|1897|6}}, they were ordered to intervene. The six destroyers became separated, and while several of them encountered groups of the German torpedo boats on their return leg, with {{HMS|Nubian|1909|6}} being badly damaged by a German torpedo and {{HMS|Amazon|1908|2}} and {{HMS|Mohawk|1907|2}} sustaining lesser damage from German gunfire, ''Cossack'' did not engage the German ships.<ref>{{cite web|last=Newbolt|first=Henry|title=History of the Great War: Naval Operations Vol. IV June 1916 to April 1917 (Part 1 of 2)| publisher=Naval-History.net|year=2013|orig-year=Originally published by Longmans, Green and Co.: London, 1928|url=http://www.naval-history.net/WW1Book-RN4a.htm|accessdate=19 December 2013}}</ref>

On 1 July 1917, ''Cossack'' collided with the transport [[SS The Duchess|SS ''The Duchess'']] near [[Eastbourne]]. ''Cossack''{{'}}s [[depth charge]]s exploded as a result of the collision, sinking ''The Duchess'' and blowing off ''Cossack''{{'}}s stern.<ref name="burt p22" /><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wrecksite.eu/wreck.aspx?10713|title=SS The Duchess [+1917]|work=www.wrecksite.eu|date=6 October 2013|accessdate=18 December 2013}}.</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ournewhaven.org.uk/page_id__207_path__0p3p57p.aspx|last=Everest|first=Ian|title=The War Dead. Part II: Newhaven 1917|work=Our Newhaven|date=24 November 2007|accessdate=18 December 2013}}</ref> ''Cossack '' was towed to Dover for repair.<ref name="baconv2 p620">{{Harvnb|Bacon|1918|p=620}}</ref>

On 16 September 1918, a fire started in one of the magazines of the newly delivered [[monitor (ship)|monitor]] [[HMS Glatton (1914)|HMS ''Glatton'']]. In order to prevent an explosion that could affect a nearby ammunition ship, it was decided to sink ''Glatton''. ''Cossack'' fired two torpedoes at ''Glatton'', one of which failed to detonate, while the second failed to defeat ''Glatton''{{'}}s [[anti-torpedo bulge]].<ref name="conways06 p47">{{harvnb|Gardiner|Gray|1985|p=47}}</ref><ref name="export p212">{{harvnb|Brook|1999|p=212}}</ref><ref name=kemp>{{harvnb|Kemp|1999|p=79}}</ref> In the end, ''Glatton'' was sunk by [[British 21 inch torpedo|21 inch (533 mm)]] torpedoes from the destroyer [[HMS Myngs (1914)|HMS ''Myngs'']].<ref name="conways06 p47"/>

''Cossack'' was sold to the [[ship breaking|shipbreakers]] [[Thos. W. Ward]] on 12 December 1919 for scrapping at their [[Preston, Lancashire]], works.<ref name="friedman p305"/><ref name="ditt p60"/>

